<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brunei Shell Petroleum</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as British Malayan Petroleum Company</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> Shell Petroleum is the main oil and gas producer of Brunei, jointly owned by the government of Brunei and the Shell group. As the British Malayan Petroleum Company, a Shell subsidiary, it discovered oil at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seria">Seria</a> in 1929, which transformed the small sultanate into one of the richest states in South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. It took its present name in 1957 and later became a joint venture with the state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brunei-shell-petroleum.jpg" alt="Brunei Shell Petroleum" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pangalau · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brunei_Shell_Petroleum_Head_Office_in_2025_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The British Malayan Petroleum Company was established on 20 July 1922 within the Royal Dutch-Shell group and registered in the United Kingdom. It bought oil-prospecting concessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> from the Asiatic Petroleum Company and Shell's Anglo-Saxon Petroleum Company, paying the Bruneian government a royalty of two shillings a ton. Early drilling at Labi, where other companies had already failed, met difficulties, and the company turned to the ground between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seria">Seria</a> and Bera rivers, where it struck oil in April 1929. Offshore exploration began in 1952.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Brunei Companies Enactment took effect on 1 January 1957, the company was reorganised and registered in Brunei, taking over operations on 15 March 1957; Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-ali-saifuddien-iii">Omar Ali Saifuddien III</a> approved the use of Brunei in its name. Its installations were targets of the TNKU rebels in the 1962 Brunei revolt. In 1963, while the Sultan was in talks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a>, the company telegraphed him news of a major find at the Southwest Ampa field, and questions over oil contributed to his decision not to join Malaysia. B.A. Hamzah has argued that the telegram was meant to sway him.</p><p>The Seria Crude Oil Terminal was opened on 5 June 1972, and in 1973 the government took a 25% stake in the company, later raised to half. Security for Shell's operations was supported by a battalion of British Gurkha troops paid for by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>, though the government denied any link between defence and the concessions. By 1997 the company held concessions covering 10,107 square kilometres, 73% of them offshore. It found oil at its deepest offshore prospect, Geronggong, in 2011, and in April 2017 made its first onshore discovery in 37 years, at the Layang-Layang well in the Seria field.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>By the late 20th century the company stood at the centre of five businesses. It handled exploration and production itself; Brunei Shell Marketing, formed in 1974, sold fuel within Brunei; Brunei LNG, founded in 1969 with Mitsubishi and the government, ran the Lumut liquefaction plant completed in 1973; Brunei Coldgas marketed the gas; and Brunei Shell Tankers shipped it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The close ties between government and company have led Brunei to be called a Shell State.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brunei Shell Petroleum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
